{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c87f","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a076ec-3023-7ad0-af14-c3080467cd16"},"sequence":666,"recorded_at":1780531400001327,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"29de407d-d987-4950-b9b4-211f15150d27","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"29de407d-d987-4950-b9b4-211f15150d27","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"29de407d-d987-4950-b9b4-211f15150d27"},"call_id":"call_01a076edb103796294540e98ba9d749d","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/STORY-260830-1i3qu7/worktree/internal/axerror/axerror.go`.\n1|// Package axerror implements the AX Structured Error contract of pinned\n2|// specification Sections 15.1 through 15.3: the closed versioned failure\n3|// object, its stable code-to-exit registry, its retryability rule, its typed\n4|// diagnostic details, and the redaction that keeps a local cause off the wire.\n5|//\n6|// The package holds no state, opens no file, and starts no process. It\n7|// advertises no provider, platform, backend, or CLI capability, and mutates no\n8|// durable state, so it has no crash or idempotency surface of its own.\n9|package axerror\n10|\n11|import (\n12|\t\"bytes\"\n13|\t\"encoding/json\"\n14|\t\"errors\"\n15|\t\"fmt\"\n16|\t\"unicode/utf8\"\n17|\n18|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/scalar\"\n19|)\n20|\n21|// ErrInvalidStructuredError reports a failure object outside the closed Section\n22|// 15.1 shape.\n23|var ErrInvalidStructuredError = errors.New(\"invalid structured error\")\n24|\n25|// IDs carries the two optional Section 15.1 identifiers. The pinned document\n26|// says they \"are optional and MUST be present when known\", which is an\n27|// obligation on the constructing call site rather than a shape a validator can\n28|// check. This type turns the obligation into a decision the call site has to\n29|// make: NoIDs states that neither identifier is known, and WithOperation and\n30|// WithSession state that one is. There is no way to leave the question\n31|// unanswered by forgetting a struct field.\n32|type IDs struct {\n33|\toperation *scalar.UUIDv7\n34|\tsession   *scalar.UUIDv7\n35|}\n36|\n37|// NoIDs states that no operation was allocated and the failure is not\n38|// session-scoped.\n39|func NoIDs() IDs { return IDs{} }\n40|\n41|// WithOperation records an allocated operation identifier.\n42|func (ids IDs) WithOperation(operation scalar.UUIDv7) IDs {\n43|\tids.operation = &operation\n44|\treturn ids\n45|}\n46|\n47|// WithSession records the session the failure is scoped to.\n48|func (ids IDs) WithSession(session scalar.UUIDv7) IDs {\n49|\tids.session = &session\n50|\treturn ids\n51|}\n52|\n53|// Operation reports the allocated operation identifier, if one is known.\n54|func (ids IDs) Operation() (scalar.UUIDv7, bool) {\n55|\tif ids.operation == nil {\n56|\t\treturn scalar.UUIDv7{}, false\n57|\t}\n58|\treturn *ids.operation, true\n59|}\n60|\n61|// Session reports the session the failure is scoped to, if one is known.\n62|func (ids IDs) Session() (scalar.UUIDv7, bool) {\n63|\tif ids.session == nil {\n64|\t\treturn scalar.UUIDv7{}, false\n65|\t}\n66|\treturn *ids.session, true\n67|}\n68|\n69|// Spec is the complete input to New. It deliberately has no exit-code field:\n70|// the exit status is resolved from the pinned registry for the selected\n71|// version, so a call site cannot mint a mapping the specification does not\n72|// assign, and no reviewer has to check one against the table by hand.\n73|type Spec struct {\n74|\t// Version is the Structured Error version the containing contract binds.\n75|\tVersion Version\n76|\t// Code is a stable registry code the selected version registers.\n77|\tCode Code\n78|\t// Message is human text of 1..4096 UTF-8 characters. Automation never\n79|\t// branches on it.\n80|\tMessage string\n81|\t// Retryable is true only when the identical request may safely be retried\n82|\t// without new authority or confirmation.\n83|\tRetryable bool\n84|\t// IDs carries the operation and session identifiers that are known.\n85|\tIDs IDs\n86|\t// Details is the required diagnostic map. An empty map is valid; nil is\n87|\t// not, because the member itself is required.\n88|\tDetails Details\n89|\t// Cause is the local Go error this failure was built from. It is kept for\n90|\t// errors.Is and errors.As and is structurally unreachable from the wire\n91|\t// object: no encoder in this package can serialize it.\n92|\tCause error\n93|}\n94|\n95|// Error is one validated Structured Error. Every field is unexported and the\n96|// only encoder is MarshalJSON, so the closed Section 15.1 object is the only\n97|// shape this type can produce and the local cause cannot escape into it.\n98|//\n99|// The details map is owned outright rather than shared. New deep-copies the\n100|// caller's map on the way in and Detail deep-copies on the way out, so the\n101|// graph ValidateDetails checked is the graph this object encodes. A shallow\n102|// copy would leave every Section 15.1 detail bound - the four forbidden\n103|// classes, the 16 KiB canonical size, the depth-4 nesting limit, the admitted\n104|// value types - violable after construction through a retained nested\n105|// container, which is a bypass path around the gate rather than a gate.\n106|type Error struct {\n107|\tversion   Version\n108|\tcode      Code\n109|\tmessage   string\n110|\texitCode  int\n111|\tretryable bool\n112|\tids       IDs\n113|\tdetails   Details\n114|\tcause     error\n115|\n116|\t// codeRegistered records whether the pinned registry carries this code for\n117|\t// this version. It is false only for an object decoded from a conforming\n118|\t// peer that used a code added in a later compatible minor.\n119|\tcodeRegistered bool\n120|}\n121|\n122|// New builds a Structured Error from spec. It refuses, in this order: an\n123|// unregistered version; a code the version does not register; a message outside\n124|// 1..4096 UTF-8 characters; a details map outside the Section 15.1 bounds or\n125|// carrying a Section 16.2 excluded class; a code whose typed details the pinned\n126|// document names and spec omits; a retryable claim the pinned document\n127|// forbids; and human text or a detail value that reproduces the local cause\n128|// verbatim.\n129|//\n130|// The exit status is never taken from the caller. It is resolved through\n131|// ExitCodeFor, so the returned object's exit_code is the exact Section 15.2\n132|// status the registry assigns to the code.\n133|//\n134|// The typed-detail requirement below is asymmetric on purpose.\n135|// target_auth_missing has exactly one declared use in Section 15.3 and that\n136|// clause names its five typed details, so the generic constructor may not emit\n137|// it with a weaker detail set than NewTargetAuthMissing produces.\n138|// capability_unavailable is a general exit-6 code whose typed details Section\n139|// 15.3 names only for the realm/broker-evidence case, so requiring them from\n140|// every capability_unavailable call site would invent a constraint the pinned\n141|// document does not state; NewRealmEvidenceUnavailable carries that\n142|// requirement for the case that does declare it.\n143|func New(spec Spec) (*Error, error) {\n144|\texitCode, err := ExitCodeFor(spec.Version, spec.Code)\n145|\tif err != nil {\n146|\t\treturn nil, err\n147|\t}\n148|\tif err := validateMessage(spec.Message); err != nil {\n149|\t\treturn nil, err\n150|\t}\n151|\tif err := ValidateDetails(spec.Details); err != nil {\n152|\t\treturn nil, err\n153|\t}\n154|\tif spec.Code == \"target_auth_missing\" {\n155|\t\tif err := requireDetailKeys(spec.Code, spec.Details, targetAuthMissingKeys); err != nil {\n156|\t\t\treturn nil, err\n157|\t\t}\n158|\t}\n159|\tif spec.Retryable {\n160|\t\tif reason, forbidden := RetryabilityRefusal(spec.Code, exitCode); forbidden {\n161|\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\n162|\t\t\t\t\"%w: %q may not claim retryable: %s\", ErrInvalidStructuredError, spec.Code, reason)\n163|\t\t}\n164|\t}\n165|\tif err := refuseCausalLeak(spec.Message, spec.Details, spec.Cause); err != nil {\n166|\t\treturn nil, err\n167|\t}\n168|\treturn &Error{\n169|\t\tversion:        spec.Version,\n170|\t\tcode:           spec.Code,\n171|\t\tmessage:        spec.Message,\n172|\t\texitCode:       exitCode,\n173|\t\tretryable:      spec.Retryable,\n174|\t\tids:            spec.IDs,\n175|\t\tdetails:        cloneDetails(spec.Details),\n176|\t\tcause:          spec.Cause,\n177|\t\tcodeRegistered: true,\n178|\t}, nil\n179|}\n180|\n181|// NewRealmEvidenceUnavailable builds the Section 15.3 capability_unavailable\n182|// failure for missing or unsafe broker, tmux server generation, or\n183|// functional-sentinel evidence, with the exact typed details that clause names.\n184|// It exists so that the realm case cannot be reported with an untyped detail\n185|// map, and so that no realm-specific code is minted while capability_unavailable\n186|// remains truthful.\n187|func NewRealmEvidenceUnavailable(version Version, message string, ids IDs, evidence RealmEvidence, cause error) (*Error, error) {\n188|\tdetails, err := evidence.Details()\n189|\tif err != nil {\n190|\t\treturn nil, err\n191|\t}\n192|\treturn New(Spec{\n193|\t\tVersion: version,\n194|\t\tCode:    \"capability_unavailable\",\n195|\t\tMessage: message,\n196|\t\tIDs:     ids,\n197|\t\tDetails: details,\n198|\t\tCause:   cause,\n199|\t})\n200|}\n201|\n202|// NewTargetAuthMissing builds the Section 15.3 target_auth_missing failure for\n203|// a missing or failed provider-auth smoke, with the exact typed details that\n204|// clause names.\n205|func NewTargetAuthMissing(version Version, message string, ids IDs, target TargetAuth, cause error) (*Error, error) {\n206|\tdetails, err := target.Details()\n207|\tif err != nil {\n208|\t\treturn nil, err\n209|\t}\n210|\treturn New(Spec{\n211|\t\tVersion: version,\n212|\t\tCode:    \"target_auth_missing\",\n213|\t\tMessage: message,\n214|\t\tIDs:     ids,\n215|\t\tDetails: details,\n216|\t\tCause:   cause,\n217|\t})\n218|}\n219|\n220|func validateMessage(message string) error {\n221|\tif !utf8.ValidString(message) {\n222|\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"%w: message is not valid UTF-8\", ErrInvalidStructuredError)\n223|\t}\n224|\tcount := utf8.RuneCountInString(message)\n225|\tif count < minMessageRunes || count > maxMessageRunes {\n226|\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\n227|\t\t\t\"%w: message is %d UTF-8 characters, the bound is %d..%d\",\n228|\t\t\tErrInvalidStructuredError, count, minMessageRunes, maxMessageRunes)\n229|\t}\n230|\treturn nil\n231|}\n232|\n233|// Version reports the Structured Error version of this object.\n234|func (failure *Error) Version() Version { return failure.version }\n235|\n236|// Code reports the stable registry code. Automation branches on this and on\n237|// ExitCode, never on Message.\n238|func (failure *Error) Code() Code { return failure.code }\n239|\n240|// Message reports the human text. It carries no machine meaning.\n241|func (failure *Error) Message() string { return failure.message }\n242|\n243|// ExitCode reports the exact Section 15.2 status carried by this object. It is\n244|// never the success status.\n245|func (failure *Error) ExitCode() int { return failure.exitCode }\n246|\n247|// Retryable reports whether the identical request may safely be retried without\n248|// new authority or confirmation. It is false for every code and exit class the\n249|// pinned document disqualifies, on both the writing and the reading side.\n250|func (failure *Error) Retryable() bool { return failure.retryable }\n251|\n252|// IDs reports the optional identifiers this object carries.\n253|func (failure *Error) IDs() IDs { return failure.ids }\n254|\n255|// CodeRegistered reports whether the pinned registry carries this object's code\n256|// for this object's version. A false result is not an error: Section 15.3\n257|// admits a code added in a compatible minor, and such an object still carries\n258|// its envelope's exit class. It is reported rather than inferred so that a\n259|// caller never mistakes an unrecognized code for a recognized one.\n260|func (failure *Error) CodeRegistered() bool { return failure.codeRegistered }\n261|\n262|// Detail returns one diagnostic value as inert data. Section 15.1 says readers\n263|// \"MUST never infer success, authority, or a remediation action from them\", and\n264|// nothing in this package does: no accessor above consults the details map, so\n265|// an unknown or adversarial detail key cannot change Code, ExitCode, Retryable,\n266|// CodeRegistered, or any identifier.\n267|//\n268|// The returned value is a deep copy: a nested object or array handed to a\n269|// caller cannot be written back into this object, so a validated failure stays\n270|// exactly the object ValidateDetails admitted.\n271|func (failure *Error) Detail(key string) (any, bool) {\n272|\tvalue, ok := failure.details[key]\n273|\tif !ok {\n274|\t\treturn nil, false\n275|\t}\n276|\treturn cloneDetailValue(value), true\n277|}\n278|\n279|// DetailKeys returns the diagnostic keys in sorted order.\n280|func (failure *Error) DetailKeys() []string { return sortedKeys(failure.details) }\n281|\n282|// Error implements the error interface. It renders the code and the human text\n283|// and never the cause, so wrapping this value with %v cannot copy untrusted or\n284|// sensitive cause text into a new message.\n285|func (failure *Error) Error() string {\n286|\treturn string(failure.code) + \": \" + failure.message\n287|}\n288|\n289|// Unwrap exposes the local cause for errors.Is and errors.As. The cause is a\n290|// local Go value: it is never encoded, and MarshalJSON has no access to it.\n291|func (failure *Error) Unwrap() error { return failure.cause }\n292|\n293|// wireError is the closed Section 15.1 object. Its field set is exactly the\n294|// nine members the pinned table declares, and the two optional identifiers are\n295|// omitted when unknown rather than emitted as null.\n296|type wireError struct {\n297|\tSchema        string  `json:\"schema\"`\n298|\tSchemaVersion Version `json:\"schema_version\"`\n299|\tCode          Code    `json:\"code\"`\n300|\tMessage       string  `json:\"message\"`\n301|\tExitCode      int     `json:\"exit_code\"`\n302|\tRetryable     bool    `json:\"retryable\"`\n303|\tOperationID   *string `json:\"operation_id,omitempty\"`\n304|\tSessionID     *string `json:\"session_id,omitempty\"`\n305|\tDetails       Details `json:\"details\"`\n306|}\n307|\n308|// MarshalJSON encodes the closed Section 15.1 object. It is the only encoder in\n309|// this package, and it can reach neither the cause nor any other local field,\n310|// so no future field added to Error can leak onto the wire without being added\n311|// here as a declared member.\n312|func (failure *Error) MarshalJSON() ([]byte, error) {\n313|\tobject := wireError{\n314|\t\tSchema:        Schema,\n315|\t\tSchemaVersion: failure.version,\n316|\t\tCode:          failure.code,\n317|\t\tMessage:       failure.message,\n318|\t\tExitCode:      failure.exitCode,\n319|\t\tRetryable:     failure.retryable,\n320|\t\tDetails:       failure.details,\n321|\t}\n322|\tif operation, known := failure.ids.Operation(); known {\n323|\t\trendered := operation.String()\n324|\t\tobject.OperationID = &rendered\n325|\t}\n326|\tif session, known := failure.ids.Session(); known {\n327|\t\trendered := session.String()\n328|\t\tobject.SessionID = &rendered\n329|\t}\n330|\tvar buffer bytes.Buffer\n331|\tencoder := json.NewEncoder(&buffer)\n332|\tencoder.SetEscapeHTML(false)\n333|\t// The encode failure below is unreachable for a validated object: every\n334|\t// member is a string, bool, int, or a details map whose values already\n335|\t// passed ValidateDetails. It is reported rather than ignored so that a\n336|\t// future member type cannot fail silently.\n337|\tif err := encoder.Encode(object); err != nil {\n338|\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: encode: %v\", ErrInvalidStructuredError, err)\n339|\t}\n340|\treturn bytes.TrimSuffix(buffer.Bytes(), []byte(\"\\n\")), nil\n341|}\n342|\n343|// cloneDetails deep-copies a validated diagnostic map. It always returns a\n344|// non-nil map, so the required details member is present in every encoded\n345|// object even when the constructing call site passed an empty one, and\n346|// MarshalJSON therefore needs no nil guard of its own.\n347|//\n348|// The copy is deep because a shallow one only isolates the top level. The\n349|// caller of New keeps whatever nested containers it passed, and a nested\n350|// container handed back by Detail is the same allocation; either one can be\n351|// written to after ValidateDetails has already run and passed.\n352|func cloneDetails(source Details) Details {\n353|\tclone := make(Details, len(source))\n354|\tfor key, value := range source {\n355|\t\tclone[key] = cloneDetailValue(value)\n356|\t}\n357|\treturn clone\n358|}\n359|\n360|// cloneDetailValue deep-copies the two container kinds a detail value may open\n361|// and returns every other admitted value unchanged. That is safe rather than\n362|// partial: validateDetailValue admits only nil, bool, string, json.Number,\n363|// []any, and map[string]any, and the four scalar forms are immutable values in\n364|// Go. Any other type is refused before a value reaches this function, so no\n365|// mutable value can fall through the default arm into a constructed object.\n366|func cloneDetailValue(value any) any {\n367|\tswitch typed := value.(type) {\n368|\tcase map[string]any:\n369|\t\tnested := make(map[string]any, len(typed))\n370|\t\tfor key, member := range typed {\n371|\t\t\tnested[key] = cloneDetailValue(member)\n372|\t\t}\n373|\t\treturn nested\n374|\tcase []any:\n375|\t\tmembers := make([]any, len(typed))\n376|\t\tfor index, member := range typed {\n377|\t\t\tmembers[index] = cloneDetailValue(member)\n378|\t\t}\n379|\t\treturn members\n380|\tdefault:\n381|\t\treturn value\n382|\t}\n383|}\n384|\n385|var _ json.Marshaler = (*Error)(nil)\n386|var _ error = (*Error)(nil)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
